the printer has acknowledged them, where its characteristic acknowledges writes). Dropping\n/// it closes it.\n@noncopy\npublic struct BluetoothLink {\n    private link: i64;\n\n    /// Connects to `device`: Classic, to its Serial Port Profile's channel; Low Energy, to the first characteristic of\n    /// a printer's service that takes writes.\n    /// Safety: the runtime reads the address during the call; the link it gives is kept and given back once (drop).\n    public fn open(device: borrow BluetoothDevice) -> Result<BluetoothLink, BluetoothError> {\n        let transport: u32 = match (device.transport) {\n            BluetoothTransport.Classic => 0,\n            BluetoothTransport.LowEnergy => 1,\n        };\n        let address = device.address.view();\n        let link = unsafe { ceesafe_bluetooth_open(transport, address.ptr, address.length as u64) };\n        if (link < 0) {\n            return Err(Bluetooth.errorOf(0 - link));\n        }\n        return Ok(BluetoothLink { link: link });\n    }\n\n    /// Writes `bytes`, all of them, in order.\n    /// Safety: the link is the runtime's, open until drop; the runtime reads the bytes during the call.\n    public fn write(borrow mut this, bytes: Slice<u8>) -> Result<void, BluetoothError> {\n        if (bytes.length == 0) {\n            return Ok();\n        }\n        let code = unsafe { ceesafe_bluetooth_write(this.link, bytes.ptr, bytes.length as u64) };\n        if (code != 0) {\n            return Err(Bluetooth.errorOf((0 - code) as i64));\n        }\n        return Ok();\n    }\n\n    /// Safety: the link is the one the runtime gave, given back once.\n    drop {\n        unsafe { ceesafe_bluetooth_close(this.link); }\n    }\n}\n",
    73 =>
        "// An allocation budget for a task and what it starts (ADR 0170; type-system.md §40.9).\n\nextern \"C\" {\n    @effects(alloc)\n    fn ceesafe_budget_enter(limit: u64) -> *mut void;\n    @effects()\n    fn ceesafe_budget_leave(budget: *mut void);\n    @effects()\n    fn ceesafe_budget_held() -> i64;\n    @effects()\n    fn ceesafe_budget_limit() -> i64;\n}\n\n/// A bound on the memory held at once by the code that runs while this value lives, and by the tasks that code\n/// starts: a request, a parser, a test.\n///\n/// ```\n/// let budget = Budget.enter(8 * 1048576)?;\n/// let answer = await handle(request)?;     // an allocation past 8 MiB fails here, as any failed allocation does\n/// ```\n///\n/// While it lives, an allocation that would take what is held over the limit fails with `AllocError.OutOfMemory`,\n/// from `push`, `Box.create`, `String.append` and the rest: the code that handles a failed allocation handles this\n/// one. What is held is the size of the blocks allocated under it and not yet freed, as the C library sizes them. A\n/// block freed under it is taken off, whoever allocated it, never below zero; a block that leaves it and is freed\n/// elsewhere stays counted. A budget entered inside another counts against both.\n///\n/// In async code the budget is the task's: it stays current across an `await`, and a task started while it is\n/// current is under it. A thread started under it is not. It is not a sandbox: memory that C libraries take for\n/// themselves is not counted. Hosted only; under `ceesafec run --interp` it holds nothing back.\n@noncopy\npublic struct Budget {\n    /// The runtime's record, as a number: the value may live in a task that moves between an executor's workers.\n    private record: usize;\n\n    /// A budget of `bytes`, current until this value is dropped. `Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory)` if there is no memory\n    /// for its record, or where the system cannot tell a block's size (under a host's allocator hook).\n    /// Safety: the runtime gives a record or null, and the record is given back once, in `drop`.\n    public fn enter(bytes: usize) -> Result<Budget, AllocError> {\n        let record = unsafe { ceesafe_budget_enter(bytes as u64) };\n        if (record.isNull()) {\n            return Err(AllocError.OutOfMemory);\n        }\n        return Ok(Budget { record: record as usize });\n    }\n\n    /// What the current budget holds now, in bytes; null outside any.\n    /// Safety: the runtime only reads the thread's current budget.\n    public fn held() -> usize? {\n        let n = unsafe { ceesafe_budget_held() };\n        return if (n < 0) null else Some(truncate<usize>(n));\n    }\n\n    /// The current budget's limit; null outside any.\n    /// Safety: as `held`.\n    public fn limit() -> usize? {\n        let n = unsafe { ceesafe_budget_limit() };\n        return if (n < 0) null else Some(truncate<usize>(n));\n    }\n\n    /// Ends the budget: the one it was entered in is current again.\n    /// Safety: the record is the one `enter` was given, given back once.\n    drop {\n        unsafe { ceesafe_budget_leave(this.record as *mut void); }\n    }\n}\n",
